.TH HD-QEMU-KVM 7 "October 2026" "hd-qemu-kvm" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-qemu-kvm \- Qemu/KVM on HyprDebian
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-qemu-kvm
installs Qemu with KVM, from
.BR qemu-system-x86 ,
the UEFI firmware OVMF and libvirt, from
.BR libvirt-daemon-system ,
and sets them up to run virtual machines on the network through the bridge
.BR br0 .
It conflicts with
.BR qemu-system-gui ,
which
.B qemu-system-x86
recommends: virtual machines are shown with
.BR virt-manager (1)
or another spice client.
.B virt-manager
is not installed with the package; install it on a desktop:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo apt install virt-manager
.fi
.RE
.SH BRIDGE
Virtual machines reach the network through the bridge
.BR br0 ,
whose ports are wired interfaces of the system.
The first installation of
.B hd-qemu-kvm
sets it up with
.BR hd-network-bridge (8),
.BR hd-network-iface (8)
and
.BR hd-network-render (8),
when the network settings of
.BR hd-network (7)
have no
.B br0
yet.
.PP
The wired interfaces are those with a device that are not wifi and not a port
of another bridge.
With one, it becomes the port of
.BR br0 .
With more, the debconf question
.B hd-qemu-kvm/bridge-ports
asks which ones; it offers the ones that the network settings configure.
More than one port joins them in one network.
Choosing none sets up no bridge.
.PP
.B br0
takes over the address settings of the first chosen interface, such as DHCP
or static addresses, and the ports lose theirs.
The bridge has a MAC address of its own, so DHCP may give it another address
than the interface had, and a connection over SSH may drop.
.PP
In a chroot, where the interfaces are those of another system, no bridge is
set up.
The network stays as it is when the package is removed.
To set up the bridge by hand, with enp3s0 as its port and DHCP:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hd-network-bridge set br0
sudo hd-network-iface set enp3s0 \-\-bridge br0
sudo hd-network-render
.fi
.RE
.SH USERS
.B hd-qemu-kvm
adds nobody to the groups
.B libvirt
and
.BR kvm .
To manage the virtual machines of
.B qemu:///system
without sudo, add your user to them and log in again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo usermod \-a \-G libvirt,kvm <user>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Members of the group
.B libvirt
control the virtual machines, and so the whole system.
.PP
New users get
.I .config/libvirt/libvirt.conf
from
.IR /etc/skel ,
which makes
.BR virsh (1)
and
.B virt-manager
connect to
.B qemu:///system
by default.
Existing users can copy it to their home directory.
.SH LIBVIRT
libvirt ships
.I /etc/libvirt/libvirtd.conf
and
.I /etc/libvirt/secret.conf
as conffiles.
.B hd-qemu-kvm
diverts them to
.I libvirtd.conf.libvirt
and
.IR secret.conf.libvirt ,
where dpkg keeps the ones of libvirt, and writes its own in their place when
it is installed, after which libvirtd is restarted:
.I libvirtd.conf
turns off listening on TCP and TLS, and
.I secret.conf
stores secrets without encryption.
Later installations leave them alone, so changes made to them stay.
Removing the package puts the ones of libvirt back.
.PP
The package also masks the sockets
.B libvirtd-tcp.socket
and
.BR libvirtd-tls.socket ,
so libvirtd only listens on its local sockets, and the drop-in
.I libvirtd.service.d/10-secret.conf
of libvirt, which hands libvirtd an encryption key for the secrets.
.SH FIREWALL
.I /usr/lib/modules-load.d/br_netfilter.conf
loads the module
.B br_netfilter
at the boot, and
.I /usr/lib/sysctl.d/50-bridge-netfilter.conf
keeps the traffic over bridges out of iptables, ip6tables and arptables, so
.BR ufw (8)
does not filter the traffic of the virtual machines on
.BR br0 .
Installing the package loads the module and applies the settings.
A file with the same name in
.I /etc/modules-load.d
or
.I /etc/sysctl.d
takes their place.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/libvirt/libvirtd.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/libvirt/secret.conf
The configs of libvirtd and its secrets driver that
.B hd-qemu-kvm
wrote.
.TP
.I /etc/libvirt/libvirtd.conf.libvirt
.TQ
.I /etc/libvirt/secret.conf.libvirt
The configs of libvirt.
.TP
.I /etc/skel/.config/libvirt/libvirt.conf
Connects virsh and virt-manager to
.BR qemu:///system ,
a conffile.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-qemu-kvm
The configs that are written to
.IR /etc/libvirt .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/modules-load.d/br_netfilter.conf
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/sysctl.d/50-bridge-netfilter.conf
Load
.B br_netfilter
and keep the traffic over bridges out of the firewall.
